<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Svetozar Pribićević</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1875">1875</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Svetozar Pribicevic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Svetozar Pribićević (1875–1936) was a Serb politician from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> who led the Croat-Serb Coalition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. In 1918 he was vice-president of the National Council of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a> and pressed for its quick union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. He served as a minister in the new kingdom, later broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>'s centralism and allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a>, and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/svetozar-pribicevic.jpg" alt="Svetozar Pribićević" width="320" height="519" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Svetozar_Pribi%C4%87evi%C4%87_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Pribićević was born on 26 October 1875 into a Serb family in Kostajnica, in the last years of the Croatian Military Frontier. He studied mathematics and physics at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-zagreb">University of Zagreb</a>, where he joined young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> in producing the periodical Narodna misao (National Thought) in 1897, which argued that Croats and Serbs were one people. In 1903 he took over the Serb People's Independent Party, and in 1905 it backed the Zadar Resolution, joining the Croatian parties of the New Course Policy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Croat-Serb Coalition, which he led from 1906, dominated Croatian politics, and Austrian and Hungarian authorities tried to break it: in 1909 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> court tried 53 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, his brothers Valerijan and Adam among them, for treason, and coalition members sued the historian Heinrich Friedjung for libel. He shared the leadership with Frano Supilo until 1910. In 1918, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> overran parts of Istria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, he urged the National Council to unite with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> without delay.</p><p>As Minister of Internal Affairs in the new Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> he applied authoritarian policies, believing strict centralism necessary to hold the union together. He was a leading figure of the Democratic Party, formed in 1920 under Ljubomir Davidović, and in December 1921 became Minister of Education. In 1924 his faction founded the Independent Democratic Party. After the 1927 election it joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatian-peasant-party">Croatian Peasant Party</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stjepan-radic">Stjepan Radić</a> in the Peasant-Democrat Coalition, and he turned against centralism, fighting the ratification of the Treaty of Nettuno with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1928.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Radić was assassinated in parliament in the summer of 1928, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> proclaimed the January 6th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">Dictatorship</a> in 1929, and Pribićević was interned at Brus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>. Released in 1931 because of his health, he emigrated. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1933 he published La dictature du roi Alexandre, blaming the king for the country's turmoil since 1918 and calling for a federal republic, and wrote a Letter to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> urging an understanding between Serbs and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> as equal nations. He died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 15 September 1936.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Svetozar Pribićević” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Svetozar Pribićević</b> led those pushing for rapid union with Serbia.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-slovenes-croats-and-serbs">State of Slovenes, Croats and Serbs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hrvatska Kostajnica, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in Podolí, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.23,16.53&amp;year=1914" aria-label="Hrvatska Kostajnica on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4943 567 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Svetozar Pribićević"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5393 837h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5393 837h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.23,16.53&amp;year=1914">What was here in 1914? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
